8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
So, let's see.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The full form of ELCB is Earth-Liquid Circuit Breaker.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
And the full form of RCCB is Residual Current Circuit Breaker.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The updated version of RCCB is ELCB.

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
And these days, it has been completely replaced by ELCB from RCCB.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
So, let's know what ELCB means first.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
ELCB is Earth-Liquid Circuit Breaker.

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Now, let's see how it works.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The green wire that we connect to ELCB is called Earth.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
And its terminal is connected through sensitive blocks.

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
So, what happens in ELCB is that incoming power comes.

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,000
A red and a black, that is, you can call it phase and neutral.

20
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
And its outgoing power is red and black, that is, phase or neutral.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
That is, phase or neutral is incoming power and its outgoing power is also there.

22
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
And that incoming power and outgoing power are connected through a sensitive tripping block.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
And that sensitive tripping block connects with the earthing wire.

24
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
If the outgoing red color wire is connected to the green color wire by accident,

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
then the red wire of the phase and the green ground wire,

26
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
the current flow in it will get leakage.

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Because of which, the tripping coil will start getting voltage drop.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,000
This voltage drop is enough to activate the tripping circuit.

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
That is, the tripping circuit will be activated by that voltage drop.

30
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
And finally, the ELCB will be open.

31
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
That is, it will be isolated from its source.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
That is, it will isolate the source from that source.

33
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Now let's understand how the function of RCCB is.

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
RCCB is a residual current circuit breaker.

35
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
RCCB works under the principle of core balance current transformer.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
So, the main function of RCCB is monitoring.

37
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
What it does is that the phase wire and the neutral wire,

38
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
how much current is flowing in it, it monitors it.

39
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Especially, the load is connected across phase and neutral terminals.

40
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
That is, in RCCB, the core balance current transformer

41
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
monitors how much current is flowing in the phase and neutral.

42
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
That is, how much current is flowing in the phase wire

43
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
and how much current is returning from the neutral wire.

44
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
That is, if the current is flowing in the phase wire,

45
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
then obviously, the same current, that is, the current of the 20 ampere,

46
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
should return from the neutral wire.

47
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
That is, the current flow of these two, which are the same,

48
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
will be zero in the core balance current transformer.

49
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
At that time, RCCB will not operate in that normal condition.

50
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Let's consider that if someone falls,

51
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
or a person touches the live phase wire.

52
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The reason for this is that the live wire and the earth,

53
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
the current flow between these two will get leakage.

54
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
That is, the existing load in it, the existing load of the phase,

55
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
was 20 mA.

56
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Now, when the person touches that phase,

57
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
the current flow in it gets leakage.

58
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
That is, the fault current in it and the leakage current

59
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
will now increase, that is, it will become 30 mA.

60
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
But our neutral wire was carrying 20 ampere.

61
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Now, when this load has increased, that is,

62
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
the 10 ampere load has increased,

63
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
the leakage current will go from another path.

64
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
That is, the path of the earth will go from that path.

65
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
So, the difference is that the first 20 ampere load

66
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
has now become 30 mA.

67
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So, this load will detect the RCCB,

68
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
that is, it will sense through CBCT,

69
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
that is, through the core balance current transformer.

70
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So, now that CBCT has sensed the current leakage,

71
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
so what it will do now is that the unbalanced current

72
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
will send the relay operating coils.

73
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
And when that relay operating coils reach,

74
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
then finally the RCCB will get dripped.

75
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
What will happen because of this is that the fault

76
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
will get isolated due to it.

77
